thinking_content: "**Analyzing Evaluation ID 38 and Charting the Next Steps**\n\nAlright, so the results are in for Evaluation ID 38: \"CNNLSTM with Increased LSTM Features (512).\" The mean solve rate clocked in at 0.452, which is slightly disappointing. It's lower than my previous best (0.462 from Evaluation ID 14), and also underperforms the RNN baseline (0.408), though I'd previously surpassed that. This tells me that simply bumping up the `lstm_features` from 256 to 512 wasn't a silver bullet. In fact, it seemed to hinder performance a bit, even with similar variance.\n\nThis is a key negative finding. It really highlights that more LSTM memory isn't always better, at least with this setup and these parameters. There's likely an optimal memory size for this architecture, or perhaps a different type of recurrent unit entirely would be more effective.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n\n1.  **Update My Internal Records:** I need to make a note of this. Evaluation ID 38's results and this analysis\
  \ will go into my private memory. It's important to track these dead ends.\n2.  **Rethinking the Architecture:** Since expanding the LSTM didn't work, I need to look at other architectural adjustments, or maybe even a more focused parameter sweep.\n    *   One thought is to further increase the CNN features. Perhaps a richer spatial representation is needed to help the agent plan more effectively.\n    *   Another avenue is a deeper LSTM - a two-layer LSTM, perhaps. But that's a significant increase in complexity.\n    *   Given the performance dip with wider hyperparameter search (Eval ID 26) and the expanded LSTM, maybe I should revisit the successful configuration from Eval ID 14 (48/96 CNN, 256 LSTM, 4e-4 LR, etc.) and explore a different tuning dimension.\n    *   It's possible it's not raw capacity, but the way information flows and is retained.\n3.  **Short-Term Focus: Deepen the CNN:** Before getting too drastic, I'll try bumping `cnn_features_1` to 64 and `cnn_features_2` to 128\
  \ (from 48 and 96), while keeping the LSTM features at 256 and sticking with the hyperparameters from the 0.462 run. This keeps me on the \"Agent architecture design\" track, tweaking one thing at a time. It's a logical next step to see if the network requires more detailed spatial input to improve planning.\n\nFirst, though, I'm going to update my internal knowledge base with these latest findings.\n"
