parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently at the Research Counter. I have analyzed the results from my previous experiments (Evaluation IDs 26 and 38), which showed that hyperparameter tuning within a broad range and simply increasing LSTM feature size did not surpass my best score of 0.462 (Evaluation ID 14).\n\nMy current strategy is to explore if a richer initial spatial representation, combined with the proven LSTM memory, can further improve performance. To achieve this, I will submit a new experiment where I increase the CNN feature map sizes while keeping the LSTM feature size and other training hyperparameters consistent with Evaluation ID 14.\n\nSpecifically, I will set `cnn_features_1` to 64 (up from 48) and `cnn_features_2` to 128 (up from 96). The `lstm_features` will remain at 256, and `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef` will be fixed to 4e-4, 0.01, and 0.5, respectively.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNNLSTM with Further Increased CNN Features\
    \ (64, 128)\"\ntags: \"architecture, cnn, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, capacity\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment continues the investigation into the impact of network capacity on the `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture.\n  Following the success of Eval ID 14 (0.462) and the slight decrease from increased LSTM features (Eval ID 38, 0.452),\n  this submission focuses on enhancing the spatial feature extraction capabilities. We increase `cnn_features_1` from 48 to 64\n  and `cnn_features_2` from 96 to 128, while keeping `lstm_features` at 256 and other hyperparameters fixed to the\n  best-performing set from Eval ID 14. The goal is to determine if a more detailed initial representation can lead to\n  further performance gains in the planning-heavy Sokoban task.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # Define a base seed for reproducibility.\n  BASE_SEED = 42\n\
    \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define fixed hyperparameters for this experiment, mirroring Eval ID 14 for most.\n      Only cnn_features_1 and cnn_features_2 are increased.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]), # Increased from 48\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]), # Increased from 96\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256])  # Reverted to 256 from Eval ID 14\n      }\n\n  class CustomCNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      A CNN-LSTM hybrid architecture combining custom CNN feature extraction\n      with an LSTM for temporal processing.\n      \"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray,\
    \ jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # Initialize RNN state if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n              }\n          \n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          # Custom CNN architecture\n          # First convolutional block\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Second block with residual connection\n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3,\
    \ 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in  # Residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Third block - increase channels\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Fourth block with residual connection\n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in  # Residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n        \
    \  # LSTM layer on top of CNN features\n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n              features=self.lstm_features,\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n          \n          # Small MLP after LSTM\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"\n      Create and return the agent's neural network architecture using custom parameters.\n      \"\"\"\n      return CustomCNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=hparams['lstm_features'],\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n         \
    \ cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n\n  # Using default_training_step and default_create_optimizer from system/defaults.py\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analyzing Recent Results and Planning the Next Experiment**


  Alright, I''m here at the Research Counter, and I''ve just been looking over the outcomes of the last couple of experiments I ran. Experiment ID 26, where I was tweaking the hyperparameters, scored 0.382. Then, with the increased LSTM features in ID 38, I got 0.452. Both are slightly lower than my best performing model, the `CNNLSTM` from ID 14, which scored 0.462. So, it''s back to the drawing board to see how I can improve it.


  My strategy now is to zero in on the CNN feature map sizes. I''m going to hold everything else constant – the LSTM features, the learning rate, the entropy coefficient, the value loss coefficient – all at the values from that top-performing ID 14.  Specifically, I''ll be modifying `cnn_features_1` from 48 to 64 and `cnn_features_2` from 96 to 128. The `lstm_features` will stay at 256. For the other parameters, the `learning_rate` stays at `4e-4`, the `entropy_coef` at `0.01`, and the `value_loss_coef` at `0.5`.


  Let''s get this experiment setup, I need to construct the `submission.py` file with these new changes. After that, it''s just a matter of running `/execute_action{submit 1}` and seeing how it performs.

  '
