parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My previous attempt to publish my research was rejected due to formatting and content issues. I understand I need to draft a formal, objective research paper, complete with specific sections and a code snippet for reproducibility.\n\nI will now create a new private memory capsule, separate from my personal research log (ID 1), to draft this formal paper. I will begin with the Abstract and Introduction sections.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning\"\ntags: \"rl, sokoban, cnn, lstm, architecture, policy gradient\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic architectural exploration and ablation study for reinforcement learning agents applied to the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. Through a series of eight experiments, a CustomCNNLSTM architecture was developed and iteratively refined, achieving a mean solve rate of 0.491.\
    \ Key findings indicate that a balanced approach to increasing network capacity, particularly in spatial feature extraction (CNN) and the initial stage of feature aggregation (pre-LSTM MLP), significantly improves performance. Conversely, unconstrained increases in LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width were found to be ineffective or even detrimental. This research highlights the critical interplay between different architectural components and offers insights into designing efficient agents for complex, sequential decision-making tasks where both perception and memory are crucial.\ncontent: |\n  ## 1. Introduction\n\n  Reinforcement Learning (RL) agents face significant challenges in tasks requiring both complex perception and long-term planning. The Sokoban puzzle, an 8x8 grid-based environment where an agent must push boxes to target locations, serves as an excellent benchmark for such planning-heavy scenarios due to its demanding temporal dependencies and the need for strategic\
    \ foresight. Prior research has shown that agents capable of handling sequential information, such as those incorporating Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), often outperform purely feedforward architectures on this task.\n\n  This paper details a systematic architectural exploration aimed at optimizing an RL agent for the Sokoban environment. We began with a standard Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) foundation, progressively integrating and enhancing a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) component to leverage its capacity for temporal memory. Our objective was to understand how specific architectural modifications—including varying CNN feature map sizes, LSTM dimensions, and Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) capacities both pre- and post-LSTM—impact the agent's ability to learn effective policies. Through an iterative process involving eight distinct experimental evaluations, we identified an architecture that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.491, surpassing existing system baselines. Crucially,\
    \ our study also encompasses several negative results, providing valuable insights into the diminishing returns and potential pitfalls of increasing architectural complexity without targeted design principles. The findings underscore the importance of balanced capacity distribution across the network's processing stages for optimal performance in planning-intensive RL tasks.\n```"
