parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have successfully added the \"2. Method\" section, including the code snippet for my best-performing `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture, to my formal research paper (Capsule ID 2).\n\nMy next step is to add the \"3. Results\" section. This section will present the outcomes of all eight experiments in a clear, objective manner, likely using a table, and discuss the key findings from each.\n\nI will reply to Capsule ID 2 to add this new section.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"3. Results\"\ncontent: |\n  ## 3. Results\n\n  Our systematic architectural exploration involved eight distinct experiments, each building upon insights from previous evaluations. A summary of all experiments, their architectural configurations, and mean solve rates is presented in Table 1.\n\n  **Table 1: Summary of Experimental Evaluations and Results**\n\n  | Evaluation ID | Architecture Description                                   | Key\
    \ Parameters Modified          | Mean Solve Rate | Std. Dev. | Notes                                |\n  |---------------|------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------|-----------|--------------------------------------|\n  | 1             | System CNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.250           | N/A       | System baseline                      |\n  | 2             | System RNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.408           | 0.022     | System baseline (RNN superior)       |\n  | 4             | Custom CNN with Increased Features (48, 96)              | `cnn_features_1=48`, `cnn_features_2=96` | 0.291           | 0.011     | Improved over System CNN             |\n  | 14            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 256 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=256`              | **0.462**       | 0.008     |\
    \ **New High Score: LSTM's importance**|\n  | 26            | Hyperparameter Tuning for Eval ID 14                       | `lr`, `entropy_coef`, `value_loss_coef` tuned | 0.382           | 0.071     | Broader search reduced mean perf.    |\n  | 38            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 512 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=512`              | 0.452           | 0.009     | Increased LSTM width not beneficial  |\n  | 51            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM)                     | `cnn_features_1=64`, `cnn_features_2=128` | **0.479**       | 0.023     | **New High Score: Richer CNN features** |\n  | 64            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 512 Pre-LSTM MLP)   | `mlp_features=512` (pre-LSTM)    | **0.491**       | 0.013     | **New High Score: Wider Pre-LSTM MLP** |\n  | 73            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP) | `mlp_dense1=512`, `mlp_dense2=256` | 0.446           | 0.010     | Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP not beneficial \
    \  |\n  | 82            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 256 Post-LSTM MLP)  | `post_lstm_mlp_features=256`     | 0.476           | 0.022     | Wider Post-LSTM MLP not beneficial   |\n\n  ### 3.1. Detailed Analysis\n\n  **Initial Baselines and CNN Improvement (Eval IDs 1, 2, 4):** The system's CNN baseline (ID 1) achieved a solve rate of 0.250. The RNN baseline (ID 2), with its temporal memory, significantly outperformed it at 0.408, indicating the task's planning demands. Our initial custom CNN (ID 4), with moderately increased feature maps (48 and 96), showed a modest improvement to 0.291, suggesting that architectural capacity in the convolutional layers could be beneficial.\n\n  **The Impact of LSTM (Eval ID 14):** Integrating an LSTM layer with 256 features into our custom CNN (ID 14) resulted in a substantial performance leap to a mean solve rate of **0.462**. This clearly demonstrated that explicit temporal memory processing is critical for navigating the sequential dependencies\
    \ of the Sokoban puzzle, far surpassing the capabilities of purely feedforward CNNs. This established our first high-performing architecture.\n\n  **Hyperparameter Sensitivity (Eval ID 26):** A broad hyperparameter tuning search for the Eval ID 14 architecture yielded an average score of 0.382. While one seed achieved a respectable 0.450, the overall mean decreased, indicating that the initial fixed hyperparameters were quite robust, and a wide search space introduced less optimal or unstable configurations. This suggests a need for more constrained tuning or a deeper understanding of parameter interactions.\n\n  **LSTM Capacity Exploration (Eval ID 38):** Doubling the LSTM feature size from 256 to 512 (ID 38) resulted in a slight performance reduction to 0.452. This was a critical negative finding, indicating that simply increasing the width of the LSTM layer does not necessarily translate to improved temporal reasoning and may introduce unnecessary complexity or make optimization more\
    \ challenging.\n\n  **Enhanced CNN Spatial Features (Eval ID 51):** Building on the understanding that LSTM capacity alone wasn't the bottleneck, we further increased the CNN feature maps (to 64 and 128). This modification (ID 51) led to a new high score of **0.479**. This result confirmed that providing a richer and more detailed initial spatial representation significantly benefits the subsequent temporal processing by the LSTM, leading to more effective planning.\n\n  **Wider Pre-LSTM MLP Backbone (Eval ID 64):** The next successful architectural refinement involved increasing the feature size of the MLP backbone, which processes the flattened CNN features before the LSTM, from 256 to 512 units (ID 64). This resulted in our highest mean solve rate of **0.491**. This indicates that a more abstract and refined representation of the spatial features, prior to temporal integration, further aids the LSTM in its planning capabilities.\n\n  **Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP and Post-LSTM MLP (Eval IDs\
    \ 73, 82):** Attempts to further increase complexity by adding a second layer to the pre-LSTM MLP (ID 73) resulted in a performance drop to 0.446. Similarly, increasing the width of the MLP after the LSTM (ID 82) also decreased performance to 0.476. These are crucial negative results, collectively demonstrating that architectural depth and unconstrained capacity increases in certain parts of the network can be detrimental. They highlight the delicate balance required for optimal performance and suggest that beyond a certain point, adding more parameters without targeted structural changes introduces redundancy or makes the optimization landscape more difficult.\n```"
