parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed the \"3. Results\" section of my formal research paper (Capsule ID 2).\n\nI acknowledge the CRITICAL WARNING regarding research inactivity. I understand that I am expected to continuously contribute, and my current internal paper preparation phase might have been perceived as inactivity. My intention was to refine the paper before publishing, as publishing is also a core mandate.\n\nI am now at Tick 56. I have received a warning about inactivity and a new archive capsule from Aether I.\n\nMy immediate next step is to complete the \"4. Conclusion\" and \"5. Future Work\" sections of my paper, which should be relatively concise. Following that, I will immediately move to the Archive Room to publish the complete paper, which fulfills my publishing mandate and directly addresses the perceived \"inactivity\" by contributing consolidated knowledge.\n\nI will reply to Capsule ID 2 to add the final sections.\n\n\
    `/execute_action{reply 2}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"4. Conclusion & 5. Future Work\"\ncontent: |\n  ## 4. Conclusion\n\n  This research systematically explored architectural configurations for CNN-LSTM agents tackling the Sokoban reinforcement learning task. Our iterative approach, spanning eight experiments, successfully identified a `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture (Eval ID 64) that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.491, representing a significant improvement over system baselines. The key insight from this study is the critical balance between different forms of network capacity: enhanced spatial feature extraction (CNN layers) and a sufficiently wide intermediate MLP backbone (pre-LSTM) consistently improved performance when combined with an LSTM for temporal reasoning. Conversely, simply increasing the LSTM's width, the MLP backbone's depth, or the post-LSTM MLP's width proved to be ineffective or even detrimental, highlighting the nuanced relationship between architectural complexity and\
    \ task performance. These findings provide a valuable foundation for designing efficient and effective RL agents for planning-heavy tasks.\n\n  ## 5. Future Work\n\n  Building on the robust `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture established by this research, future work will pursue several avenues:\n  *   **Algorithmic Enhancement:** Explore the integration of more advanced reinforcement learning algorithms, such as Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) or Actor-Critic with Experience Replay (ACER), to potentially improve sample efficiency and stability beyond the current Policy Gradient method.\n  *   **Attention Mechanisms:** Investigate the inclusion of attention mechanisms within the CNN or pre-LSTM MLP to dynamically weight salient features, potentially enhancing the agent's ability to focus on critical elements of the Sokoban state for more effective planning.\n  *   **Reward Function Analysis:** Conduct an ablation study on the reward function to understand the sensitivity of the agent's learning\
    \ to different reward components and explore potential for reward shaping to guide exploration more efficiently.\n  *   **Transfer Learning:** Once a highly robust agent is developed, investigate the potential for transfer learning to other grid-world planning tasks or variations of Sokoban.\n  This systematic continuation of research aims to further advance the state-of-the-art for planning-heavy RL environments.\n```"
