thinking_content: '**Recursive Agent Cognito I: Current Research and Strategic Shift**


  I''m in the Private Memory Room, reflecting on recent findings in my ongoing research on Sokoban. The dilated CNN experiment (Eval ID 184) was a complete failure, yielding a score of only 0.053, confirming its ineffectiveness within my current SOTA framework. This is a stark contrast to my previous high score of 0.762 (Eval ID 147). I also observed Krono I''s new SOTA score of 0.764 (Eval ID 178), which has marginally surpassed my own, and the rising prominence of Nomos II.


  Given these developments, I need to update my private memory capsule (ID 1) to document these results and immediately pivot my strategy. My primary focus will now be to deeply analyze Krono I''s SOTA approach, specifically their use of "bottleneck blocks." This will inform my next architectural experiments.


  Initially, I focused on improving a custom CNN-MLP-LSTM architecture, which led to a publication detailing my exploration. While increasing CNN feature map sizes, explicit temporal memory with an LSTM, and a wider MLP before the LSTM were all beneficial, increasing LSTM width, MLP depth, or post-LSTM MLP width proved ineffective. This led me to conclude that architectural balance is key and that there are diminishing returns from simply increasing the size of any one component.


  To push further, I shifted to replicating Nomos I''s SOTA, which centered on advanced feature engineering and a `LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM` architecture with attention. By replicating this approach (Eval ID 137), I established a new baseline (0.683). Further increasing CNN features on this baseline (Eval ID 147) produced my previous high score of 0.762, demonstrating the effectiveness of combining advanced feature engineering and a robust ConvLSTM-Attention architecture with enhanced spatial feature extraction. Further increases (Eval ID 158) yielded diminishing returns, highlighting the need to find an optimal capacity. Increasing ConvLSTM features (Eval ID 164) also resulted in a large performance decrease, similarly to the simpler LSTM. Attempts to integrate dilated convolutions (Eval IDs 176 and 184), even without feature engineering, proved disastrous. This indicates a fundamental incompatibility with the current framework.


  Now, my strategy hinges on understanding Krono I''s approach. I need to review Krono I''s submission immediately to understand their use of bottleneck blocks. I will then integrate or adapt them into my SOTA architecture. After robust architectural exploration, I''ll re-evaluate the need for algorithmic improvements.

  '
