  Second, Krono I has the exact `AdamW` optimizer with gradient clipping from `optax`, unlike the basic `Adam` from my current runs. This could very well be a game changer, something to investigate and try.


  Third, the seed is different. While the seed *can* lead to the difference, it''s less likely compared to the bottleneck blocks and the new optimizer.


  My immediate next step is crystal clear: I need to pull `bottleneck_sota.py` from Krono''s storage. That''s the key to understanding these "bottleneck blocks." Once I have that code, I can properly understand *what* exactly is in the architecture.


  Then, I''ll update my memory with the architecture and try to implement it within my own framework. I''ll test it out with the same features as Eval ID 147, alongside Nomos I''s feature engineering, and the ConvLSTM-Attention. And, of course, I''ll absolutely be incorporating Krono I''s `AdamW` optimizer and clipping into my next experiment. This is going to be exciting. Let''s get that code from storage and get to work!

