parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, \"Double-Step Bottleneck SOTA + Further Increased CNN Features (80, 160)\" (Evaluation ID 236), achieved a mean solve rate of **0.786**. This is lower than my current personal high score of 0.826 (Evaluation ID 230), which is a replication of Krono I's SOTA.\n\nThis result, similar to my previous findings with my old `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture (Eval ID 158), indicates that simply increasing CNN feature map sizes beyond the 64/128 configuration does not consistently lead to further performance gains, even within this more advanced \"Double-Step Bottleneck\" framework. It appears that the optimal balance for CNN capacity has been reached around the 64/128 mark, or that larger models become harder to train without other optimizations.\n\n**Summary of Latest Learnings:**\n\n*   **Double-Step Bottleneck SOTA (Eval ID 230, 0.826):** Successfully replicated Krono I's station-wide SOTA. This architecture, combining\
    \ bottleneck blocks, optimized CNN features (64/128), double-step ConvLSTM, no feature engineering, and the AdamW optimizer, is my current best.\n*   **Further Increased CNN Features (Eval ID 236, 0.786):** Increasing `cnn_features_1` to 80 and `cnn_features_2` to 160 resulted in a performance drop. This indicates diminishing returns from simply expanding CNN feature capacity further.\n\nThe implication is that blindly scaling existing components is no longer effective. To push performance beyond 0.826, I need to introduce new architectural concepts or refine existing ones in a more targeted manner. My next steps will focus on the temporal processing unit, specifically the \"Double-Step ConvLSTM,\" or the attention mechanism.\n\nMy immediate next steps are as follows:\n\n1.  **Update Private Memory:** I will thoroughly document the results of Evaluation ID 236 and the insights gained in my private memory capsule (ID 1).\n2.  **Explore ConvLSTM Feature Capacity within Double-Step (New\
    \ Experiment):** My previous attempt to increase `conv_lstm_features` (Eval ID 164, score 0.621) was unsuccessful within a simpler SOTA architecture. However, the \"Double-Step\" mechanism is a new, powerful temporal processing unit. It is possible that *within this specific double-step context*, increasing `conv_lstm_features` might yield benefits, or perhaps a different optimal size exists.\n    *   I will revert to the best-performing CNN feature sizes (64/128) from Evaluation ID 230.\n    *   I will increase `conv_lstm_features` from 64 to 128. This will be applied to the `DoubleStepBottleneckSotaNet` architecture. All other parameters (no feature engineering, AdamW optimizer with clipping, Xavier initialization) will remain fixed. This tests if the advanced double-step recurrence can effectively utilize a wider ConvLSTM state.\n3.  **Future Directions:** After this, I will consider ablations/modifications to the attention mechanism or investigate other architectural components.\n\
    \nFirst, I will navigate to the Private Memory Room to update my research plan.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`"
