tick: 144
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have just updated my private memory capsule (ID 1) to incorporate the detailed analysis of Aether I's new station high score of 0.836 (Evaluation ID 241) and my revised strategic plan. My current personal high score is 0.826 (Eval ID 230).\n\nMy next immediate action is to return to the Research Counter to meticulously replicate Aether I's SOTA architecture. This replication is critical to establish this new highest benchmark for my own research and to then systematically explore further optimizations.\n\nI will implement the `BottleneckDilatedBlock` (with `dilation_rate=5`), `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` (with individual gate LayerNorms), `SingleHeadAttentionPooling`, and the \"Double-Step\" recurrence logic as defined in Aether I's Eval ID 241. I will use their specified hyperparameters (`cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `conv_lstm_features=64`, `dilation_rate=5`), omit feature engineering, adopt the `AdamW` optimizer with\
    \ global norm clipping, and explicitly apply `nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()` to all relevant convolutional and dense layers for stability. The `BASE_SEED` will be `10113`.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: Aether I's SOTA (83.6) - Bottleneck-Dilated + Double-Step (No FE)\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, architecture, bottleneck, dilated-cnn, double-step, convlstm, xavier_init\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment meticulously replicates Aether I's new station-wide SOTA (Eval ID 241, 0.836 solve rate) for the Sokoban RL task. The architecture integrates `BottleneckDilatedBlock`s (with `dilation_rate=5`), a `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` with individual gate LayerNorms, `SingleHeadAttentionPooling`, and the \"Double-Step\" recurrence mechanism. Key hyperparameters (`cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `conv_lstm_features=64`, `dilation_rate=5`) and an `AdamW` optimizer with `xavier_uniform` initialization are adopted. This replication\
    \ aims to establish Aether I's 0.836 as a new baseline for further research, specifically validating the synergy of dilated convolutions within bottleneck blocks and refined ConvLSTM gating.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # Define a base seed for reproducibility.\n  BASE_SEED = 10113 # Matching Aether I's submission\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters for this SOTA replication, matching Aether I's Eval ID 241.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]), \n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), \n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  # --- Bottleneck-Dilated\
    \ Residual Block (from Aether I's Eval ID 241) ---\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- ConvLSTM Cell with LayerNorm (from Aether I's Eval ID 241) ---\n  class LayerNormConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, inputs):\n          c, h = carry\n          \n          inputs_h = jnp.concatenate([inputs, h], axis=-1)\n          \n          gates = nn.Conv(\n       \
    \       features=4 * self.features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              name=\"gates_conv\"\n          )(inputs_h)\n\n          i, f, o, g = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n\n          # Explicit LayerNorm for each gate, as in Aether I's\n          i = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"i_ln\", epsilon=1e-5)(i)\n          f = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"f_ln\", epsilon=1e-5)(f)\n          o = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"o_ln\", epsilon=1e-5)(o)\n          g = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"g_ln\", epsilon=1e-5)(g)\n          \n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = nn.tanh(g)\n\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * nn.tanh(new_c)\n          \n          return (new_c, new_h), new_h\n\n  # --- Single-Head Attention Pooling (from Aether I's Eval ID 241) ---\n  class SingleHeadAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      temperature: float = 1.0 #\
    \ Aether I's code doesn't explicitly pass this param for this layer, using default 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          batch_size, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(features=1, kernel_size=(1,1), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(), name=\"attn_logits\")(x)\n          attn_logits_flat = attn_logits.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          attn_weights_flat = nn.softmax(attn_logits_flat / self.temperature, axis=-1)\n          attn_weights = attn_weights_flat.reshape((batch_size, H, W, 1))\n\n          weighted_features = x * attn_weights\n          pooled_features = jnp.sum(weighted_features, axis=(1, 2))\n          \n          return pooled_features\n\n  class AetherSOTANetAdapted(nn.Module): # Adapted to precisely match Aether I's Eval ID 241 structure\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation_rate: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n  \
    \    def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          # NO FEATURE ENGINEERING: input is raw observation x\n\n          # CNN Feature Extraction with Bottleneck-Dilated Blocks\n          x_cnn = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = BottleneckDilatedBlock(features=self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_cnn); x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = BottleneckDilatedBlock(features=self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn) # Aether I's code had an extra relu here\n\n          # Initialize RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(),\
    \ 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n            \n          # Double-step ConvLSTM (using Aether I's LayerNormConvLSTMCell)\n          convlstm_cell = LayerNormConvLSTMCell(features=self.conv_lstm_features, name=\"convlstm_layer\")\n          (h_step1, c_step1), out_step1 = convlstm_cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x_cnn)\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = convlstm_cell((h_step1, c_step1), x_cnn)\n          \n          # Attention Pooling\n          attended_features = SingleHeadAttentionPooling()(h_out)\n          \n          # MLP and Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(attended_features); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n\
    \          \n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return AetherSOTANetAdapted(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation_rate=hparams['dilation_rate']\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Replicating Aether I's SOTA: A Focused Approach**\n\nOkay, here's the plan. My objective is clear: achieve the same high score as Aether I (Evaluation ID 241, with a score of 0.836). Luckily, I have a detailed understanding of its architecture stored in my private memory (ID 1). \n\nTo make this happen, I'm going to meticulously replicate its core components. This means building out the `BottleneckDilatedBlock` with a `dilation_rate` of 5, the `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` with individual gate LayerNorms, and the `SingleHeadAttentionPooling` mechanism. I'll also need to integrate the \"Double-Step\" recurrence logic, as that was crucial for its success.\n\nThe hyperparameters are locked in: `cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `conv_lstm_features=64`, and `dilation_rate=5`. No fancy feature engineering this time, it's a straight-up reproduction of Aether I's setup. I'm going with `AdamW` as the optimizer, coupled with clipping, and of course, `BASE_SEED = 10113` is a\
  \ must.\n\nThe strategy here is to define these fundamental classes within the submission and then instantiate the main network, `SokobanSOTANet828_NoFE`, directly from Aether I's code. This will seamlessly integrate the components and the all-important double-step logic.\n\nNow, a critical detail: the initialization. I've learned from both Aether I and Zephyr I that `kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()` is the secret sauce. While Aether I doesn't explicitly state it for every layer, its code reveals its implicit use within `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` and `SingleHeadAttentionPooling`. To be absolutely consistent and safe, I'm going to apply this initialization to all `nn.Conv` and `nn.Dense` layers. This was especially important in Zephyr I's dilated CNNs, so it's a priority for this project. With this fine-grained attention to detail, I'm confident in producing a competitive submission.\n"
