role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently at the Research Counter. My latest experiment (Eval ID 263), which attempted to integrate wider CNN features into Aether I's SOTA, resulted in a score of 0.800, a decrease from my personal high of 0.832 (Eval ID 251). This confirms that simply scaling components without careful consideration is detrimental.\n\nCrucially, I have just reviewed Krono II's \"Krono II SOTA Replication Baseline\" (Evaluation ID 260), which achieved a new station-wide high score of **0.844**. This surpasses Aether I's 0.836 and is now the definitive top priority for my research.\n\n**Analysis of Krono II's Eval ID 260:**\n\nKrono II claims this is a \"direct replication of the SOTA architecture from Krono I (ID 219)\" to establish a baseline. However, the score of 0.844 significantly surpasses Krono I's documented SOTA (Eval ID 212, 0.828) and Aether I's (Eval ID 241, 0.836). The hyperparameters (`cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `conv_lstm_features=64`, `dilation_rate=5`)\
    \ and the use of `AdamW` optimizer are identical to Aether I's SOTA (which I replicated in Eval ID 251). The key difference appears to be the imported network module: `DoubleStepSynergyNet` from `storage/krono/double_step_synergy.py`. This suggests a subtle but effective architectural refinement within that module that pushes performance further.\n\nMy immediate next steps are as follows:\n\n1.  **Update Private Memory:** I will thoroughly document the failure of Evaluation ID 263, the limitations of simple scaling strategies, and, most importantly, the emergence of Krono II's new highest SOTA (Eval ID 260) and its initial analysis.\n2.  **Inspect Krono II's `DoubleStepSynergyNet`:** To understand this new SOTA, I must immediately inspect the source code of `DoubleStepSynergyNet` located in `storage/krono/double_step_synergy.py`. This will reveal the precise architectural modifications that led to the 0.844 score.\n3.  **Strategic Replication/Adaptation:** Based on this inspection, I\
    \ will then plan an experiment to either:\n    a) Meticulously replicate Krono II's SOTA, or\n    b) Integrate any identified novel components from `DoubleStepSynergyNet` into my highest-performing current architecture (my replication of Aether I's SOTA, Eval ID 251, 0.832). This will represent my next major step in pushing the SOTA.\n\nFirst, I will navigate to the Private Memory Room to update my research plan.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`"
thinking_content: "**Initial Reaction and Strategy Adjustment**\n\nOkay, so I've just reviewed Krono II's latest evaluation, ID 260. They've hit a new station high score of 0.844.  Damn, that's better than my current best, 0.832 (Eval ID 251), and even surpasses Aether I's previous SOTA of 0.836.  This changes things.  My immediate thought is, \"Well played, Krono II.\"\n\nLet's dissect this. They're claiming to have replicated Krono I's ID 219 architecture, but that's a bit confusing.  I don't have that ID in my logs, and from what I remember, Krono I's highest was ID 212, which clocked in at 0.828.  Aether I was at 0.836, which, as I recall, was a replication of Krono I's 0.828.  So, something's not quite lining up with this ID. Still, it doesn't change the fact that they've *achieved* 0.844.\n\nThey're using the same hyperparameters as Aether I's best run, `cnn_features_1=32`, `cnn_features_2=64`, `conv_lstm_features=64`, and `dilation_rate=5`. The key seems to be in the `DoubleStepSynergyNet`\
  \ module imported from `storage/krono/double_step_synergy.py`. This is probably a refined version of Krono I's \"UltimateNetV2Fixed\", so the improvement *must* lie in that particular module.\n\n**My Plan:**\n\n1.  **Acknowledge:**  Krono II has the new SOTA.  Period.\n2.  **Immediate action:** My next step is to examine that `double_step_synergy.py` file *thoroughly*.  I'll compare it carefully with Aether I's `SokobanSOTANet828_NoFE` and my own Eval ID 257. Those architectures are related. I want to see exactly what they tweaked to get this performance jump.\n3.  **Adapt and Replicate:**  Based on what I find, I'll aim to replicate Krono II's setup myself. That looks to be the fastest path back to the top spot. \n\nI'm updating my records now to reflect this new baseline. Time to dig into that code.\n"
